WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.120
PROFESSOR: Welcome, all,
to the fifth lecture

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.750
of the fall lecture series, God
and Computers, minds, machines,

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:22.070
and metaphysics.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:24.270
Let me first announce
like I always do,

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:27.450
we have a discussion
group going on in parallel

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:30.330
to this lecture
series, which is run

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.660
by Professor Harvey Cox from
Harvard Divinity School and me.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.570
And we meet the next
time on November 24

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:39.060
at Harvard Divinity
School at 12 o'clock.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:42.810
Harvard Divinity School
is at 45 Francis Avenue

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.310
over at Cambridge.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:47.260
For more information,
please give me an email.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:49.638
My email address is
annef@ai.mit.edu.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:57.860
When you visit my homepage
or the AI lab event page,

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:00.140
you can also find the
abstracts for all the talks

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:01.120
in the series.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:06.770
And please feel free to visit
and to go around and watch.

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:09.050
Just to give people
who are today here

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:11.180
the first time a
little hint where

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.730
we are right now
in the series, we

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:17.150
have started with four lectures
on where we kind of had

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:19.820
from different angles,
from an engineer's angle,

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:24.290
a mythology angle, a
neuroscientist angle, and an AI

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:26.390
angle what the human factor is.

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:27.710
What human intelligence is.

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:30.752
What makes us special or
what makes us not special.

00:01:30.752 --> 00:01:32.210
We are right now
in the second part

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:35.090
of the series, where we have
four different points of view

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:38.450
where scientists present more
or less personal accounts on how

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:41.660
they deal with
existential issues

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:43.730
within and outside
their research.

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:46.400
Last week, we had a
very personal account

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:49.820
from a Christian perspective
done by Federico Girosi, who

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:51.500
is here at MIT.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:54.350
Today we will hear a
Buddhist perspective.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:57.920
Next week, we will have
a Jewish perspective.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.580
And in two weeks, we will
have a Hindu perspective.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:02.720
All on these questions.

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:09.960
And I'm now very
happy, very glad,

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:16.960
and very proud to welcome
Francisco Varela, who

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.840
is currently
director of research

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:23.140
at the Laboratory of Cognitive
Neuroscience in the National

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:26.440
Center for Scientific
Research in Paris.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:29.690
He studied, first, medicine at
the University of Santiago de

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:32.540
Chile, and later,
biology at Harvard, where

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:36.380
he received his PhD in 1970.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:38.600
He went later back
to Chile but was

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.670
forced to leave after
the putsch and went

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:42.950
to Boulder, where
he became director

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:47.240
of epistemological research
at the Naropa Institute.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.300
What fascinates me
specifically about his work,

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:54.290
that he really always combines
strict empirical research

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:56.330
with philosophical reasoning.

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:59.180
And so he was
therefore enormously

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:02.780
influential in both current
epistemological theories

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:04.970
and neuroscientific research.

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:07.460
He even was influential
for technology

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:10.130
because Rodney Brooks, the
director of the iLab, who

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:13.190
gave his talk two weeks
ago, bases his technology

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:16.160
on Francisco Varela's work.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:17.540
He wrote numerous books.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:20.270
I just want to mention two,
which is The Tree of Knowledge,

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:25.130
from 1987, and The
Embodied Mind from 1992.

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:28.100
And started-- and this is
also one of the reasons

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:30.350
why I invited him today--
many years ago, he

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:35.030
started to work intensively
together with the Dalai Lama.

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:40.130
He just edited a book called
Sleeping, Dreaming, and Dying,

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:43.850
an Exploration of Consciousness
with the Dalai Lama,

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:45.740
and published
several other books

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:48.470
on Buddhism and neuroscience.

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:51.110
And I'm very glad and very
happy that you came all the way

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:54.140
from Paris to share with us your
thoughts on cognitive science

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:54.921
and Buddhism.

00:03:54.921 --> 00:03:55.420
Welcome.

00:04:04.793 --> 00:04:06.376
FRANCISCO VARELA:
Thank you very much.

00:04:06.376 --> 00:04:08.270
It is a real pleasure.

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:14.620
I am not sure that I can live up
to your very generous depiction

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:16.890
of what I can or
can possibly do.

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:21.790
I am going to be quite a
bit more modest in what

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:23.720
I would like to
share with you today,

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:27.010
which is that to address
the question that

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:32.140
is behind this entire
series, god and computers,

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:33.610
as I said in my
abstracts, I would

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:36.160
like to put myself
very comfortably

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:37.090
right in the middle.

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:39.760
Because I don't
believe particularly

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:47.380
in a theistic or god position,
nor am I a computer expert.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:50.450
However, what I would
like to bring in,

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:54.230
and that's maybe where the
Buddhist influence does show up

00:04:54.230 --> 00:05:02.600
in me, is that I do very
strongly pursue and hold

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:05.840
to the position that
human experience,

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:11.810
simple, ordinary, lived
everyday, direct, first-person

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:14.850
human experience, is essential.

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:18.150
And it's essential, and
that's the main point today.

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:22.680
For me, both-- and there
is no contradiction here,

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:24.630
as far as I can see--

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:32.090
both for the study of science,
the scientific study of mind

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:37.460
today and for a
quest for meaning,

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:40.520
significance in our
own existence, which

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:44.770
is what I consider
spirituality, which

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:49.600
is very much the take of
the tradition of Buddhism.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:53.860
So the challenge
for me today is then

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:57.370
to try and point out
a couple of points

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:00.640
where I see that these
two perspectives,

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:02.890
human experience
being at the center,

00:06:02.890 --> 00:06:07.120
how does it enter
necessarily, inescapably,

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:12.400
into a good, modern cognitive
neuroscience or cognitive

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:16.960
science, and at the same
time, how properly understood,

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:20.980
this opens up necessarily
or inescapably also

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:24.400
into a quest for human
values and meaning

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:30.460
that is a religious or
spiritual dimension.

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:35.250
So let me start with experience.

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:38.080
Now, as you know, I'm
not going to bore you

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:41.510
with a lot of background,
because it's been in the media.

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:44.050
It's been in the literature.

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:47.740
The question of
consciousness, first-person

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:52.600
or human experience
within the modern science

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:55.960
or modern cognitive
neuroscience, I should say,

00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:59.290
has been flaming up recently.

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:00.480
Oodles of books.

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.880
There's sort of a book
boom on the topic.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:06.040
The way I like to see
it is by going back

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:08.530
to a very interesting
way of phrasing it

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:11.800
by somebody from MIT,
Roy Jackendoff, who

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:14.680
many years ago, published
a very pioneering book

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:17.470
on consciousness and
the computational mind.

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:19.250
And he phrased the
question by saying,

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:25.480
well, you have a computational
mind, a process of sorts,

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:27.520
then you have a
body and a brain.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:31.520
That kind of circulation,
we can get our hands on.

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:33.290
That's just good science.

00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:36.340
The real issue here
is the relationship

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:38.350
between the
computational mind and

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:39.940
the fundamental logical mind.

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:43.360
That is, here, the
technical term phenomenology

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:46.660
throughout my lecture,
please understand it

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:51.090
as being synonymous with direct
access to human experience.

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:51.610
OK?

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:54.690
So I'm going to use the Western
tradition of phenomenology,

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:55.530
i.e.

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:59.110
post Husserlian phenomenology,
in the same sense

00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:01.510
as in the Buddhist
tradition, one actually

00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:04.840
addresses direct experience
by direct examination

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:08.140
rather than by any
other possible means.

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:10.540
And as Jackendoff pointed
out, there is really

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:13.750
not a mind-body problem,
there is a mind-mind problem.

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:18.320
The computational
phenomenological issue.

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:23.870
Now the main point that I
would like to sketch for you

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:33.210
today is that what we need, and
it is not so difficult to find,

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:39.120
is to build a passage
or a circulation

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:43.740
between these two forms
of mind, the computational

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:46.470
and the phenomenological mind.

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:52.170
Instead of considering
them as absolutely opposite

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:56.380
or impossible to blend
or forever apart--

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:59.760
what is the famous words,
the twain shall never meet?

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:03.550
The point here is that yes,
not only they can meet,

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:06.930
they are, in fact, two
aspects of the same reality.

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:11.490
But in order to really make
that productive beyond words,

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:16.880
we need to avail ourselves with
new tools, new perspectives,

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:20.190
and particularly new methods.

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:24.950
The two points that are central
for me is on the one hand

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:28.110
is to actually
understand how can we

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:32.760
build an active circulation
between the computational

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:35.160
or biological or
neurobiological side

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:36.900
and the phenomenological side.

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:38.610
That's going to
be the first part

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:41.170
of what I'm going to say today.

00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:44.260
And the second
essential resource

00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:48.370
we need to put back onto the
table that has been thoroughly

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:50.410
neglected, certainly
within science,

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:52.990
and by a large in the
Western tradition,

00:09:52.990 --> 00:09:56.500
is a method for
examination of experience

00:09:56.500 --> 00:10:00.070
that is a proper
phenomenological method.

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:05.110
So both things are going to
lead me into, hopefully in time,

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:08.490
the end of this talk.

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:12.260
Now, please bear with me.

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:15.170
It's going to be,
I think, I hope,

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:18.490
a relatively tight argument that
I'm going to try to develop.

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:19.900
There is not much time.

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:29.210
So please bear with me if it
is a little bit too fast track.

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:32.570
As it's fashioned today.

00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:37.190
Let me just first of
all start by giving

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:40.940
an example of the
kinds of issues

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:42.920
that come up when
you ask the question,

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:44.200
is there a circulation.

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:46.820
So this is chapter
1, circulation

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:49.130
or reciprocal,
mutual circulation

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:54.350
between phenomenological
and neurocomputational mind.

00:10:54.350 --> 00:10:56.240
What kinds of issues
do we find here?

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:57.500
Just to fix ideas.

00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:00.740
Just so that this
is not so vague.

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:05.900
I just bring this one, which is
one particularly illuminating.

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:08.870
Again, understand this
just as a mere example,

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:11.450
not as a demonstration
on anything.

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:15.650
Everybody knows the
famous Necker cube.

00:11:15.650 --> 00:11:20.960
You perhaps don't know, but
there is interesting literature

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:23.400
that shows that if you
look into the Necker cube,

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:27.380
it is a bi-stable perception
that shifts back and forth.

00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:29.700
And it takes time to
shift back and forth.

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:35.150
And in fact, it goes from
front to back and back to front

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:38.090
with certain periodicity,
which is not like a clockwork,

00:11:38.090 --> 00:11:40.730
but it has a certain frequency.

00:11:40.730 --> 00:11:45.500
And in fact, here, for
example, you have in the lines,

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:48.560
indicate the moment when
the perceiver actually

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:50.060
sees the flip.

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:52.320
And it just indicates
that with a button,

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:54.540
so you have a time series.

00:11:54.540 --> 00:11:58.820
The interesting thing
here is that when you now

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:02.690
keep doing the same experiment,
but you change perspectives,

00:12:02.690 --> 00:12:05.030
you do an easy
Necker cube and you

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:08.120
move into a more difficult
Necker cube, that is,

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:12.230
by changing the angle of
rotation, what you have

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:15.530
is a very different
distribution of this interval,

00:12:15.530 --> 00:12:19.500
of the inter-shift
intervals, as indicated here.

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:22.970
This one for this one,
this one for that one.

00:12:22.970 --> 00:12:27.290
Now, if you have any
kind of familiarity

00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:31.400
with the neural
basis of these sorts

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:33.770
of bistable visual
perceptions, you

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:37.550
know that this is a
classical demonstration

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:40.620
of the dynamical quality
of a process that

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:43.020
is relatively bistable.

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:44.810
There's quite a bit
of knowledge known

00:12:44.810 --> 00:12:47.120
about the circuitry behind it.

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:50.720
Last year, a beautiful paper
by a couple of researchers,

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:53.180
Leopold and
Logothetis, even showed

00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:55.970
how, in fact, recording
from single neurons

00:12:55.970 --> 00:13:00.080
could anticipate the flip by
a few hundred milliseconds.

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:04.070
And in fact, when you
study the dynamics

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:06.110
of these kinds of
neuron and events,

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:10.140
you do find this kind
of dynamical setting.

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:14.160
Here, the setting is given
by choosing your perspective.

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:17.400
So precisely that's the point.

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:22.140
A global, perceptual,
experiential perspective.

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:24.030
That is, how you see the cube.

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:27.660
And it does you, the first
person, who is seeing the cube,

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:32.490
is setting up a context
of the underlying base,

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:35.190
the neuronal
machinery if you want,

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:40.690
so that you actually influence
that local neuronal machinery,

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:44.730
and that's reflected in
the rate of flip-flop.

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:47.610
And in fact, in the case of
the Logothetis experiment

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:50.340
with monkeys, the
expectation of the monkey

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:53.430
could influence how
the rate of discharge

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:57.520
of certain neurons in the
visual cortex would occur.

00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:00.960
So that's the kind of flavor
that I want to convey.

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:04.320
The example is meant to
just give you the sense

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:06.860
that it's hard for
me to understand--

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:08.580
and I honestly say--
it is hard for me

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:14.730
to understand why is it so hard
to understand that when you

00:14:14.730 --> 00:14:24.620
have a complete perceptual,
cognitive, conscious or aware

00:14:24.620 --> 00:14:27.440
experience, that is, you're
looking at the Necker cube,

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:28.730
this is not separable.

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:32.870
In fact, it is a mutual up,
down, or downwards and upwards

00:14:32.870 --> 00:14:35.690
causation with
the actual details

00:14:35.690 --> 00:14:38.390
of the local computations.

00:14:38.390 --> 00:14:41.180
And that the circulation
between the two not

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:45.590
only should not be seen as
an impossibility, in fact,

00:14:45.590 --> 00:14:48.090
we see it all the time.

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:53.480
Now, the usual argument
to deny this is--

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:58.580
by the way, this is what I
like to call the research

00:14:58.580 --> 00:15:01.470
project of neurophenomenology.

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:04.160
Neurophenomenology
is just a key word

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:08.780
that I'm throwing out to mean
precisely how can we actually

00:15:08.780 --> 00:15:11.450
constructively build a
research project which

00:15:11.450 --> 00:15:17.180
has yet to be fully fleshed
out, between these two levels.

00:15:17.180 --> 00:15:19.310
That is, the
phenomenological level

00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:23.900
and the neurocomputational
level in active passages,

00:15:23.900 --> 00:15:27.590
both upwards, from the
neuro into the conscious

00:15:27.590 --> 00:15:32.780
or into the phenomenological,
and also reciprocally,

00:15:32.780 --> 00:15:35.240
backwards from the
phenomenological,

00:15:35.240 --> 00:15:39.090
constraining the
local phenomena.

00:15:39.090 --> 00:15:54.660
Now, the usual claim
today from people

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:59.460
who oppose the idea of
such a project, and I'm

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:01.590
going to take two
minutes to say this,

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:04.680
because it's really crucial
to crack what I think

00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:09.090
is sort of an optical illusion
of the issue of experience

00:16:09.090 --> 00:16:13.800
of phenomenological mind in the
field of cognitive neuroscience

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:16.710
is that in the current
debate of consciousness,

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:19.500
there is a large
majority of people who

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:23.910
would oppose the existence
of this first-hand experience

00:16:23.910 --> 00:16:27.660
or first-person experience,
the phenomenological level,

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:30.360
by choosing either
most typically,

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:33.930
a form of reductionism,
saying ultimately,

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:36.550
we can do away
with consciousness

00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:39.430
or with experience as some
sort of epiphenomenon.

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:41.160
It's really not
that interesting,

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:44.040
except as a correlate
of something else.

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:47.900
Or the more moderate ones,
also a very popular position,

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:50.190
a la Dennett and
few others, would

00:16:50.190 --> 00:16:52.040
be some form of functionalist.

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:56.220
That is, it doing something,
but in fact, in itself, we

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:58.560
don't have, possibly,
any access to it,

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:00.840
nor is it something
that can be active

00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:03.790
within scientific research.

00:17:03.790 --> 00:17:08.940
So this is just to situate
my position that here, we

00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:11.940
have a whole constellation of
people working in this domain,

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:16.170
where in fact, the claim
is quite the opposite.

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:21.020
The argument is usually
that since we know,

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:26.109
and it's true, that there are
very precise neural correlates

00:17:26.109 --> 00:17:30.160
or neural bases for a number
of cognitive phenomenological

00:17:30.160 --> 00:17:33.890
processes, such as perception,
motion, and so on and so forth,

00:17:33.890 --> 00:17:37.300
therefore, that's the
only one that counts.

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:39.910
Now, that, of
course, only proves

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:43.060
that there are some
objects of research

00:17:43.060 --> 00:17:44.980
that are at that level.

00:17:44.980 --> 00:17:48.400
It doesn't prove that it is
the only object of research,

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:52.270
that it is the interesting one.

00:17:52.270 --> 00:17:56.800
The third-person
position, as it is usually

00:17:56.800 --> 00:18:01.270
described, this being the
first-person position,

00:18:01.270 --> 00:18:04.390
makes-- is an interesting
slippage of sense

00:18:04.390 --> 00:18:08.110
by saying that because there
are levels which are reducible,

00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:10.300
therefore everything
is reducible.

00:18:10.300 --> 00:18:12.040
It's a funny kind of shift.

00:18:12.040 --> 00:18:13.810
All I'm saying, it's
something that to

00:18:13.810 --> 00:18:16.690
me is almost common
sense, which is to say,

00:18:16.690 --> 00:18:18.580
there are some things
that are reducible,

00:18:18.580 --> 00:18:21.100
there are other
things that are not.

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:22.900
There are some levels
of descriptions

00:18:22.900 --> 00:18:25.390
that I admit that kind of
put downward projection,

00:18:25.390 --> 00:18:27.610
but there are some that are not.

00:18:27.610 --> 00:18:31.240
And the whole issue is to find
the right level of description,

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:34.570
depending on what
are you studying.

00:18:34.570 --> 00:18:40.190
Not everything can be
studied at the same level.

00:18:40.190 --> 00:18:49.080
Basic advantage, I would say,
of the position I'm defending

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:53.410
is actually quite
obvious, because we all

00:18:53.410 --> 00:19:02.210
have this strange, fascinating
[CHUCKLES] thing we carry

00:19:02.210 --> 00:19:03.590
around, which is the body.

00:19:06.260 --> 00:19:09.460
And every time we look
at the body, every time

00:19:09.460 --> 00:19:12.310
I move my arm,
every time I touch,

00:19:12.310 --> 00:19:17.090
every time I look and take
this glass, this gesture.

00:19:17.090 --> 00:19:21.000
And it is only within the
realm of the bonding experience

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:25.560
that this happens, that you have
two irreducible facts together,

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:27.250
inseparably together.

00:19:27.250 --> 00:19:30.090
On the one hand, there
is the motion of the arm.

00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:32.330
There is all of the
external description

00:19:32.330 --> 00:19:34.980
that I can do of
how this is done.

00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:39.240
At the same time, and
also as part of the data

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:42.210
that phenomena is
proposing to me,

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:48.140
I have my experience of seeing
the glass and drinking it.

00:19:48.140 --> 00:19:52.560
Again, all I'm saying is
that these two sets of data

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:55.500
need to go together, and
they are irreducible.

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:57.730
There are some levels
at which they touch.

00:19:57.730 --> 00:19:59.940
There are some levels
at which they do not.

00:19:59.940 --> 00:20:02.550
If you ask me, do I
have an experience,

00:20:02.550 --> 00:20:06.810
do I have a possible
access, conscious access,

00:20:06.810 --> 00:20:12.330
to the discharge of the neuron
125 of the motor cortex,

00:20:12.330 --> 00:20:13.890
probably not.

00:20:13.890 --> 00:20:19.470
But if I have access to
the perception of the glass

00:20:19.470 --> 00:20:22.860
or the actual flip-flop
of the Necker cube,

00:20:22.860 --> 00:20:24.645
the answer seems to be yes.

00:20:28.390 --> 00:20:29.730
OK.

00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:34.260
So the body, to
me, provides what

00:20:34.260 --> 00:20:39.780
I consider the most
irreducible source of arguments

00:20:39.780 --> 00:20:45.182
for the inseparability of
these two kinds of phenomenon.

00:20:45.182 --> 00:20:54.260
Now, in order to move just one
step more into the direction

00:20:54.260 --> 00:20:58.250
I want to go, I
need to again shift

00:20:58.250 --> 00:21:04.290
back into a mode
of illustration, so

00:21:04.290 --> 00:21:09.510
that we move away from the very
general statements into some--

00:21:09.510 --> 00:21:12.690
you have the flavor, a specific
whiff of the kinds of things

00:21:12.690 --> 00:21:17.060
that I try to do
in the laboratory.

00:21:17.060 --> 00:21:20.780
How does this articulation of
these two irreducible levels

00:21:20.780 --> 00:21:23.620
actually work out?

00:21:23.620 --> 00:21:27.190
Now, here, one of
the topics that

00:21:27.190 --> 00:21:30.760
is most interesting
to illustrate what

00:21:30.760 --> 00:21:37.090
I'm trying to do is, in fact,
a more profound and a more

00:21:37.090 --> 00:21:41.810
constitutive event than the
flip-flop of a Necker cube,

00:21:41.810 --> 00:21:44.220
which is time.

00:21:44.220 --> 00:21:47.670
And therefore, let me
just use as illustration

00:21:47.670 --> 00:21:53.670
the neurophenomenology of
present-time consciousness.

00:21:53.670 --> 00:21:58.900
How is the time
now actually seen

00:21:58.900 --> 00:22:03.300
from this neurophenomenological
perspective.

00:22:03.300 --> 00:22:06.550
So in order to do
that, it makes sense

00:22:06.550 --> 00:22:09.260
we have to see a
phenomenological side,

00:22:09.260 --> 00:22:13.000
and we have to see
the neural side.

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:14.050
So let me start.

00:22:14.050 --> 00:22:18.160
Again, time here is playing
the role of one of those events

00:22:18.160 --> 00:22:22.600
where, by having a body, these
two things are mixed together.

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:26.620
This is not one of those objects
that it is easy to reduce,

00:22:26.620 --> 00:22:31.360
nor is it one of those
phenomenological events

00:22:31.360 --> 00:22:35.350
that has no link to
actual neural substrates

00:22:35.350 --> 00:22:38.080
or bodily substrate.

00:22:38.080 --> 00:22:41.020
But the first
contribution has to come--

00:22:41.020 --> 00:22:43.210
or if you will start with
a contribution coming

00:22:43.210 --> 00:22:48.040
from phenomenology,
we hit upon what

00:22:48.040 --> 00:22:53.560
I would say is already the
announcement, the opening

00:22:53.560 --> 00:22:57.200
into my second
message here, which

00:22:57.200 --> 00:23:01.680
is that you say, what is now?

00:23:01.680 --> 00:23:08.360
And we all understand how you
experience grabbing a cup.

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:11.390
But now you ask yourself
the question, what is now?

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:14.990
How do you experience right now?

00:23:14.990 --> 00:23:18.970
What is the nature
of that experience?

00:23:18.970 --> 00:23:22.350
Well, in fact, there
are many examples

00:23:22.350 --> 00:23:25.170
in the literature you can read,
for example, the famous paper

00:23:25.170 --> 00:23:28.650
by Ginsberg and Dennett, where--

00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:33.420
or in the book by Roy
Jackendoff himself,

00:23:33.420 --> 00:23:36.630
where the whole issue is
simply taken by saying,

00:23:36.630 --> 00:23:40.560
oh, time is just this point.

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:42.350
And there is a little duration.

00:23:42.350 --> 00:23:44.490
There is a little
glob around it,

00:23:44.490 --> 00:23:46.230
but basically,
that's what it is.

00:23:48.740 --> 00:23:53.690
What phenomenology--
and please, if you

00:23:53.690 --> 00:23:57.350
would like to take something
home from what I'm saying,

00:23:57.350 --> 00:24:00.500
this might be one of the
things that you would

00:24:00.500 --> 00:24:03.150
like to take home and consider.

00:24:03.150 --> 00:24:05.210
One of the messages
from phenomenology,

00:24:05.210 --> 00:24:10.610
both Western and Buddhist,
is the observation

00:24:10.610 --> 00:24:16.400
that when it comes to the
study of experience, just

00:24:16.400 --> 00:24:21.690
a casual, cursory
inspection is not enough.

00:24:21.690 --> 00:24:26.410
It is not enough to simply
say, oh, I'll just look.

00:24:26.410 --> 00:24:30.040
Because when you
just look, in fact,

00:24:30.040 --> 00:24:32.920
you pretend to be an
expert of something

00:24:32.920 --> 00:24:34.780
which you've never done.

00:24:34.780 --> 00:24:37.960
As if by listening
to sounds, you

00:24:37.960 --> 00:24:41.050
would be automatically
a musician.

00:24:41.050 --> 00:24:44.750
Or by having a body,
you would be a doctor.

00:24:44.750 --> 00:24:48.420
That, of course, is nonsense.

00:24:48.420 --> 00:24:53.430
For example of time it's
particularly beautiful,

00:24:53.430 --> 00:24:58.110
because when you do
an analysis of time,

00:24:58.110 --> 00:25:01.590
and I'm taking this now straight
from the phenomenological

00:25:01.590 --> 00:25:04.590
tradition of the
Western philosophy,

00:25:04.590 --> 00:25:07.830
in this case, the
work of Husserl.

00:25:07.830 --> 00:25:11.790
The late work of Husserl
and a few other contributors

00:25:11.790 --> 00:25:13.560
later on.

00:25:13.560 --> 00:25:16.620
The structure of
present time turns out

00:25:16.620 --> 00:25:21.600
to be an extremely complex
and interestingly layered

00:25:21.600 --> 00:25:23.140
phenomenon.

00:25:23.140 --> 00:25:27.240
In fact, in particular,
not only does it have what

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:30.360
many people, including
the casual inspection of

00:25:30.360 --> 00:25:34.860
say, a la Ginsberg and Dennett
shows, that the present--

00:25:34.860 --> 00:25:36.810
or William James,
for that matter.

00:25:36.810 --> 00:25:40.230
The idea of the species
present that he beautifully

00:25:40.230 --> 00:25:43.170
describes in chapter
12, I think it is,

00:25:43.170 --> 00:25:45.210
of The Principle of Psychology.

00:25:45.210 --> 00:25:49.030
Why did he call it
the species present?

00:25:49.030 --> 00:25:54.500
It is because it has this
funny quality of having

00:25:54.500 --> 00:25:57.080
a center and a periphery.

00:25:57.080 --> 00:25:59.810
It is not like a
continuous line.

00:25:59.810 --> 00:26:04.020
It has a center and periphery,
much like the visual foveal

00:26:04.020 --> 00:26:07.820
and peripheral vision
in the visual system.

00:26:07.820 --> 00:26:12.290
But that center periphery
has, in itself, a structure,

00:26:12.290 --> 00:26:17.930
which is partly what he called
the retentional threads, which

00:26:17.930 --> 00:26:21.830
make what I see still part of
the present without invoking

00:26:21.830 --> 00:26:25.290
memory, which is still
another phenomena.

00:26:25.290 --> 00:26:27.660
That's already an
interesting distinction.

00:26:27.660 --> 00:26:30.690
And at the same
time, the opening

00:26:30.690 --> 00:26:34.740
or the setting of the
boundary conditions for what

00:26:34.740 --> 00:26:36.930
is going to come next.

00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:40.740
That's what Husserl called
the static or transversal

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:43.080
intentionality, or the
three-part structure

00:26:43.080 --> 00:26:45.030
of temporality.

00:26:45.030 --> 00:26:47.670
That three-part
structural temporality

00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:54.270
is actually inseparable from the
non-discontinuous non-central

00:26:54.270 --> 00:26:59.760
periphery or continuous flow
of the possibility of time,

00:26:59.760 --> 00:27:03.280
where these moments
of time emerge

00:27:03.280 --> 00:27:05.940
which you can call the flow.

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:10.020
And in fact, when you examine
the longitudinal intentionality

00:27:10.020 --> 00:27:12.120
of time or the
genetic constitution

00:27:12.120 --> 00:27:14.700
of time, what you
find is that it

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:18.840
is inseparable from affective
dispositions or affect

00:27:18.840 --> 00:27:21.840
or emotional tone sets.

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:24.120
Now, you can say, how
do you know all of that?

00:27:24.120 --> 00:27:26.490
Well, I'm not
demonstrating it to you,

00:27:26.490 --> 00:27:30.630
but there is all kinds of
published and to be published

00:27:30.630 --> 00:27:35.790
and ongoing research, like
in any other literature, that

00:27:35.790 --> 00:27:39.060
will point to the
kinds of observations

00:27:39.060 --> 00:27:42.810
that you can do to
provide, to stuff up,

00:27:42.810 --> 00:27:46.470
to actually give the ground
for these kinds of conclusions.

00:27:46.470 --> 00:27:50.760
And I'm just bringing
this as an example how,

00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:56.970
by applying in detail, very
precise analyses of experience

00:27:56.970 --> 00:28:01.050
that does not show up
under cursory inspection,

00:28:01.050 --> 00:28:03.210
you can come up with
a structure that

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:05.580
is both surprising
and interesting.

00:28:05.580 --> 00:28:09.480
Central periphery with
these dynamical retentions

00:28:09.480 --> 00:28:11.400
of the just passed
and the opening

00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:13.770
into the future and
the constitution

00:28:13.770 --> 00:28:17.980
through affect and
the permanent flow.

00:28:17.980 --> 00:28:21.100
At the same time, these
kinds of observation

00:28:21.100 --> 00:28:25.610
can be mixed, with no,
at all, immediately

00:28:25.610 --> 00:28:35.030
suggested the flip side of
what neuroscience can tell us.

00:28:35.030 --> 00:28:38.350
Because when you do a
third-person perspective--

00:28:38.350 --> 00:28:41.110
and then, again,
I'm now going back

00:28:41.110 --> 00:28:46.730
to what comes from the
cognitive neuroscience.

00:28:46.730 --> 00:28:52.370
it has been known for many
years that time, indeed,

00:28:52.370 --> 00:28:55.720
has the behavior of animals.

00:28:55.720 --> 00:29:00.530
Ethologists know this,
and it is part of research

00:29:00.530 --> 00:29:05.750
that you can see, even
in the old days of EEG,

00:29:05.750 --> 00:29:12.050
that time comes in globs which
lasts about 500 milliseconds.

00:29:12.050 --> 00:29:13.940
It's a rough estimate.

00:29:13.940 --> 00:29:16.700
In other words, it's
a perceptual frame,

00:29:16.700 --> 00:29:19.400
what it takes to
constitute a perception

00:29:19.400 --> 00:29:21.000
or a moment of behavior.

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:23.570
For example, here,
when an animal

00:29:23.570 --> 00:29:26.690
has an orienting reaction,
you turn your head,

00:29:26.690 --> 00:29:29.960
and you foveate, because
you hear a sound.

00:29:29.960 --> 00:29:34.730
And then the next moment,
once you reorient and foveate,

00:29:34.730 --> 00:29:37.350
I recognize a face.

00:29:37.350 --> 00:29:39.960
Neurobiologically,
it has been known

00:29:39.960 --> 00:29:43.410
that takes about
that kind of time.

00:29:43.410 --> 00:29:46.860
And again, I cannot
go into details.

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:50.610
What is interesting is
that recently, there

00:29:50.610 --> 00:29:55.080
is a very interesting idea
how that description can

00:29:55.080 --> 00:29:57.990
be provided with
a mechanism of how

00:29:57.990 --> 00:30:00.990
that moment, those moments
of cognitive present,

00:30:00.990 --> 00:30:04.860
arise through a specific
mechanism, which

00:30:04.860 --> 00:30:09.150
is a process of very
fast synchronization

00:30:09.150 --> 00:30:13.350
or temporal coherence in
very distributed brain

00:30:13.350 --> 00:30:17.340
ensembles or brain assemblies,
as is sometimes said,

00:30:17.340 --> 00:30:21.000
that might be dispersed
throughout the nervous system.

00:30:21.000 --> 00:30:24.570
And it is one of the current
hypotheses much studied

00:30:24.570 --> 00:30:28.350
by many labs today,
including ourselves,

00:30:28.350 --> 00:30:32.670
how that kind of
synchronicity, which

00:30:32.670 --> 00:30:38.220
is based on an actual
electrical oscillation,

00:30:38.220 --> 00:30:42.720
can provide the glue for
distributed assemblies

00:30:42.720 --> 00:30:48.720
to become what is under the
constitution of this behavior,

00:30:48.720 --> 00:30:51.960
such as foveation
and recognition.

00:30:51.960 --> 00:30:56.040
And you can observe that,
just to make things, again,

00:30:56.040 --> 00:30:58.440
concrete.

00:30:58.440 --> 00:31:02.700
These are some recent
data from my lab.

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:05.610
By looking at,
say, human patients

00:31:05.610 --> 00:31:09.300
that have electrodes
implanted into their brains,

00:31:09.300 --> 00:31:11.180
because they're
epileptic, and they're

00:31:11.180 --> 00:31:14.070
waiting epileptic resection.

00:31:14.070 --> 00:31:16.890
Now when you do
that, these people

00:31:16.890 --> 00:31:20.820
are willing, sometimes,
to work with us

00:31:20.820 --> 00:31:25.240
and provide answers to
simple cognitive experiments,

00:31:25.240 --> 00:31:30.300
such as discrimination of
visual objects or simple working

00:31:30.300 --> 00:31:31.800
memory tasks.

00:31:31.800 --> 00:31:33.960
But at the same time,
we have a whole bunch

00:31:33.960 --> 00:31:35.740
of electrodes in this case--

00:31:35.740 --> 00:31:38.340
about 24 electrodes--
distributed

00:31:38.340 --> 00:31:40.380
throughout their brain,
and we can actually

00:31:40.380 --> 00:31:44.700
study this exact
process of emergence

00:31:44.700 --> 00:31:47.220
of their experience
of the discrimination

00:31:47.220 --> 00:31:50.280
while they are doing
it under the hypothesis

00:31:50.280 --> 00:31:52.590
that what we are
observing is titrating

00:31:52.590 --> 00:31:56.040
this constitution of the present
through the establishment

00:31:56.040 --> 00:31:57.670
of synchrony.

00:31:57.670 --> 00:32:01.590
Again, this is just
a way of giving you

00:32:01.590 --> 00:32:07.470
a little whiff of the
story of how you can then

00:32:07.470 --> 00:32:12.360
proceed to investigate
neurophenomenologically

00:32:12.360 --> 00:32:14.500
by putting these
two things together.

00:32:14.500 --> 00:32:16.980
In other words, the
person actually engaged

00:32:16.980 --> 00:32:18.780
in the experience
with his own account,

00:32:18.780 --> 00:32:22.300
his own phenomenology,
and the results

00:32:22.300 --> 00:32:24.900
of the recordings
and the phenomena

00:32:24.900 --> 00:32:28.860
that give rise to that
moment of experience,

00:32:28.860 --> 00:32:33.670
this is just the kind
of results you can get.

00:32:33.670 --> 00:32:35.660
Excuse me, it's complicated.

00:32:35.660 --> 00:32:39.580
But you will see in a
minute, it's not that bad.

00:32:39.580 --> 00:32:42.730
These are four
electrodes implanted

00:32:42.730 --> 00:32:45.520
in a particular
patient, two that

00:32:45.520 --> 00:32:47.770
go all the way from
the back of the head,

00:32:47.770 --> 00:32:51.790
through the limbic system,
into the head of the amygdala.

00:32:51.790 --> 00:32:54.550
And these other two
in the frontal that

00:32:54.550 --> 00:32:58.670
go through the frontal lobes,
down into the cingulate cortex.

00:32:58.670 --> 00:33:01.000
So what you have
here, every point

00:33:01.000 --> 00:33:04.120
here represents widely
distributed places

00:33:04.120 --> 00:33:05.480
in the brain.

00:33:05.480 --> 00:33:09.340
And you can see that this
patient, while he's recognizing

00:33:09.340 --> 00:33:13.240
an illusory contour, in this
case, either recognizing it

00:33:13.240 --> 00:33:16.920
or not, when there
is a red line,

00:33:16.920 --> 00:33:20.500
we have drawn a
red line where you

00:33:20.500 --> 00:33:24.940
can find a statistically
very robust synchrony

00:33:24.940 --> 00:33:27.280
between these very
distant places.

00:33:27.280 --> 00:33:31.120
In this case, for example,
the anterior hippocampus

00:33:31.120 --> 00:33:35.020
and the anterior cingulate gyrus
of the other side of the brain.

00:33:35.020 --> 00:33:36.270
Far apart.

00:33:36.270 --> 00:33:37.270
OK?

00:33:37.270 --> 00:33:39.010
Doesn't happen everywhere.

00:33:39.010 --> 00:33:41.350
There are some local
phenomena. in other words,

00:33:41.350 --> 00:33:43.870
adjacent places of
recording, and it does

00:33:43.870 --> 00:33:46.570
happen between distant places.

00:33:46.570 --> 00:33:47.170
Some of you--

00:33:47.170 --> 00:33:49.700
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:49.700 --> 00:33:50.200
No.

00:33:50.200 --> 00:33:53.070
In this case are local
field potentials,

00:33:53.070 --> 00:33:54.320
because they are small.

00:33:54.320 --> 00:33:59.390
Maybe a few millimeters
in diameter local fields.

00:33:59.390 --> 00:34:00.830
You could do the
same thing if you

00:34:00.830 --> 00:34:03.750
had access to the cells,
which has been done,

00:34:03.750 --> 00:34:07.310
as you perhaps know.

00:34:07.310 --> 00:34:10.100
Thanks for the clarification.

00:34:10.100 --> 00:34:13.455
I know that those of you who
are not into this kind of field,

00:34:13.455 --> 00:34:14.580
this doesn't mean anything.

00:34:14.580 --> 00:34:20.449
Just take it as an example
of the sort of thing

00:34:20.449 --> 00:34:23.510
that can be done
if you are working

00:34:23.510 --> 00:34:26.880
in the lab with this
kind of material.

00:34:26.880 --> 00:34:27.770
Good.

00:34:27.770 --> 00:34:32.600
What does this buy us in terms
of where we're trying to go?

00:34:32.600 --> 00:34:35.929
Well, the main point
I'm trying to set now,

00:34:35.929 --> 00:34:42.120
back to the general
issue, is that when

00:34:42.120 --> 00:34:46.250
we think about the
relationship between the two--

00:34:46.250 --> 00:34:47.400
the mind-mind gap.

00:34:47.400 --> 00:34:52.260
The phenomenology and
the computational mind,

00:34:52.260 --> 00:34:54.480
there are many ways
in which from--

00:34:54.480 --> 00:34:57.330
and I'm going to move now from
what I consider more or less

00:34:57.330 --> 00:35:00.690
trivial into more
or less interesting

00:35:00.690 --> 00:35:03.480
ways of establishing
the connection.

00:35:03.480 --> 00:35:06.530
The most classical, the
most reductionistic way

00:35:06.530 --> 00:35:07.830
is of this kind.

00:35:07.830 --> 00:35:10.290
You can find it in any
psychology textbook

00:35:10.290 --> 00:35:12.330
you care to examine.

00:35:12.330 --> 00:35:14.820
Where if you have
phi, here, it's

00:35:14.820 --> 00:35:16.980
some kind of
neurobiological terms,

00:35:16.980 --> 00:35:20.760
say synchronicity between
distant brain sizes and brain

00:35:20.760 --> 00:35:21.720
sites.

00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:24.330
And psi are some
phenomenal terms,

00:35:24.330 --> 00:35:27.990
such as I see the
illusory contour.

00:35:27.990 --> 00:35:30.720
And then the argument
is usually this

00:35:30.720 --> 00:35:33.330
is a formulation
due to David Taylor,

00:35:33.330 --> 00:35:39.360
it says phi looks like psi,
therefore it explains it.

00:35:39.360 --> 00:35:43.020
So the synchronicity looks
like the constitution

00:35:43.020 --> 00:35:46.260
of a moment of perception,
therefore that's it.

00:35:46.260 --> 00:35:47.700
You've got it.

00:35:47.700 --> 00:35:50.940
Now, that, again, would
violate the reducibility

00:35:50.940 --> 00:35:54.630
that I was referring to before.

00:35:54.630 --> 00:36:01.210
A little better is what you
might call a more soft path.

00:36:01.210 --> 00:36:04.620
That's the hard
path, if you want.

00:36:04.620 --> 00:36:08.350
A more soft path
consists of just

00:36:08.350 --> 00:36:13.510
pointing at what you might call
a phenomenal isomorphism, not

00:36:13.510 --> 00:36:17.770
an analytical isomorphism,
where you establish

00:36:17.770 --> 00:36:21.030
the parallels, such
as synchronous cell

00:36:21.030 --> 00:36:23.690
assemblies, temporal
retention, for example.

00:36:23.690 --> 00:36:24.190
OK?

00:36:24.190 --> 00:36:26.170
We're back to the
example I just mentioned.

00:36:26.170 --> 00:36:32.050
Or efference copy in the motor
system and voluntary action.

00:36:32.050 --> 00:36:38.050
Or the perception of the other
and this beautiful, recently

00:36:38.050 --> 00:36:44.950
described mirror neurons
in the cortex described

00:36:44.950 --> 00:36:49.840
by [INAUDIBLE],, where the neuron
fires when the monkey moves

00:36:49.840 --> 00:36:53.140
the arm, but it also fires just
as much when another monkey

00:36:53.140 --> 00:36:55.450
moves the arms in front of him.

00:36:55.450 --> 00:36:55.950
OK?

00:36:55.950 --> 00:36:58.130
That's why he calls
them mirror neurons.

00:36:58.130 --> 00:36:59.540
And you say, oh, wow.

00:36:59.540 --> 00:37:00.120
That's great.

00:37:00.120 --> 00:37:05.230
So that is the neuronal basis
of the perception of another?

00:37:05.230 --> 00:37:08.630
Again you can take that into
the linking preposition mode

00:37:08.630 --> 00:37:13.040
and say, that's it, or
as many people do today,

00:37:13.040 --> 00:37:15.680
and this is again
referring to the literature

00:37:15.680 --> 00:37:17.930
as it's going on, they've
taken more soft pass,

00:37:17.930 --> 00:37:19.510
and say, well, we don't know.

00:37:19.510 --> 00:37:21.190
There is some kind
of isomorphism.

00:37:21.190 --> 00:37:24.770
This is the old
idea of the duality,

00:37:24.770 --> 00:37:28.850
the neurophenomenal duality.

00:37:28.850 --> 00:37:31.910
And you leave that with
big question marks,

00:37:31.910 --> 00:37:33.710
and you stop there.

00:37:33.710 --> 00:37:37.190
Well, what I'm trying to
propose is that you can actually

00:37:37.190 --> 00:37:43.850
bridge that by building active
constraints in both directions.

00:37:43.850 --> 00:37:45.800
And this is how it would go.

00:37:45.800 --> 00:37:48.560
You would take
phenomenological data,

00:37:48.560 --> 00:37:52.610
but again, if you avail
yourself with the right kind

00:37:52.610 --> 00:37:55.760
of precision that is
required and the right kind

00:37:55.760 --> 00:37:59.810
of fundamental
adequacy of the method

00:37:59.810 --> 00:38:04.080
to unfold what is present
within experience,

00:38:04.080 --> 00:38:07.530
that gives rise to some
descriptive invariance,

00:38:07.530 --> 00:38:10.200
and I'll come back to
that when I go back

00:38:10.200 --> 00:38:11.950
to method, in a moment.

00:38:11.950 --> 00:38:16.150
In other words, the
data in itself is deaf,

00:38:16.150 --> 00:38:20.400
but you can actually describe
that into patterns, such

00:38:20.400 --> 00:38:25.090
as you saw in the case of
time, with the longitudinal

00:38:25.090 --> 00:38:26.490
transversional intentionality.

00:38:26.490 --> 00:38:29.970
That's a pattern that comes
from many, many observations.

00:38:29.970 --> 00:38:34.620
You build an invariance, and
into a formal description that

00:38:34.620 --> 00:38:39.280
lends itself, because it is an
invariant and a description,

00:38:39.280 --> 00:38:42.090
to some models and
descriptions, such

00:38:42.090 --> 00:38:43.780
as you can use in this case.

00:38:43.780 --> 00:38:47.580
It's very adequate
dynamical models,

00:38:47.580 --> 00:38:52.650
which again leads itself into
naturalistic implementations,

00:38:52.650 --> 00:38:53.910
which could be artificial.

00:38:53.910 --> 00:38:57.660
In this case, the
implementation is neuronal.

00:38:57.660 --> 00:39:02.520
So there you have a way of
actually not just saying

00:39:02.520 --> 00:39:04.540
these two things are
next to each other,

00:39:04.540 --> 00:39:08.190
but of actually building
the necessary passages that

00:39:08.190 --> 00:39:09.790
connect one with the other.

00:39:09.790 --> 00:39:13.530
For example, you could say,
if you take this perspective

00:39:13.530 --> 00:39:16.620
that you have to
immediately discard

00:39:16.620 --> 00:39:21.310
any form of naturalistic
implementation of time, that

00:39:21.310 --> 00:39:25.230
would be a continuous time
flow, as in a classical computer

00:39:25.230 --> 00:39:29.010
system, that would be absolutely
out from the beginning,

00:39:29.010 --> 00:39:32.130
if you respect the fact that
this phenomenological data

00:39:32.130 --> 00:39:38.250
constrains the way the actual
machinery could or should work.

00:39:38.250 --> 00:39:41.610
Now of course, that's not
necessarily a problem,

00:39:41.610 --> 00:39:43.650
because it is
immediately obvious

00:39:43.650 --> 00:39:46.540
that that's not the case.

00:39:46.540 --> 00:39:50.580
So again, to summarize
that point again,

00:39:50.580 --> 00:39:56.460
it's like saying that neural
phenomenology, in this sense,

00:39:56.460 --> 00:40:00.000
has what I like to
describe as a triple braid.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:02.940
On the one hand, you
need a form of precision

00:40:02.940 --> 00:40:07.510
to make the passage possible
between the two descriptions.

00:40:07.510 --> 00:40:11.890
The second is that you need to
find the kinds of processes,

00:40:11.890 --> 00:40:15.220
and in the case at hand, it
is the process of synchrony

00:40:15.220 --> 00:40:17.680
that would be the
right candidate, which

00:40:17.680 --> 00:40:21.700
is situated at the junction
between the phenomenological

00:40:21.700 --> 00:40:23.920
level and the
biological sources.

00:40:23.920 --> 00:40:27.310
You shouldn't go to, say,
membrane properties, which are

00:40:27.310 --> 00:40:29.920
in themselves fundamental data.

00:40:29.920 --> 00:40:31.690
But you shouldn't
go to that level,

00:40:31.690 --> 00:40:36.260
because it wouldn't be
helpful for the junction.

00:40:36.260 --> 00:40:40.290
And then third, and
I'm back to this point,

00:40:40.290 --> 00:40:43.840
the explicit and pragmatic
methodology of examination

00:40:43.840 --> 00:40:48.280
where the lived experience
and the actual bodily base

00:40:48.280 --> 00:40:51.760
interpenetrate.

00:40:51.760 --> 00:40:53.560
OK.

00:40:53.560 --> 00:40:55.510
Take a breath.

00:40:55.510 --> 00:40:58.180
We're going to the
next part, which

00:40:58.180 --> 00:41:02.060
is you're going to ask
me, I hope, at this point,

00:41:02.060 --> 00:41:03.490
so how do you do it?

00:41:03.490 --> 00:41:06.580
This is all great,
but how do you do it?

00:41:06.580 --> 00:41:09.050
So far you've said
you can do it.

00:41:09.050 --> 00:41:13.150
Now, I'm going to spend the
next 10 or so minutes trying

00:41:13.150 --> 00:41:17.410
to say a few things about
what is it that you do

00:41:17.410 --> 00:41:21.370
and how that leads into the
examination of experience

00:41:21.370 --> 00:41:25.260
in the way we need
to touch upon here.

00:41:25.260 --> 00:41:36.630
Now, first of all, let me
say that the whole question

00:41:36.630 --> 00:41:38.790
of the phenomenology
of experience, and I've

00:41:38.790 --> 00:41:42.000
said it before, is
really a blind spot

00:41:42.000 --> 00:41:43.170
in the Western tradition.

00:41:43.170 --> 00:41:50.950
So to me, it's really an
absolute fantastic discovery

00:41:50.950 --> 00:41:54.116
to find that many
of my colleagues,

00:41:54.116 --> 00:41:55.490
when they write
about experience,

00:41:55.490 --> 00:41:59.520
can be so scholarly and
so precise and so creative

00:41:59.520 --> 00:42:02.400
when it comes to describe
naturalistic data,

00:42:02.400 --> 00:42:04.080
but can be so
superficial when it

00:42:04.080 --> 00:42:06.270
comes to describe experience.

00:42:06.270 --> 00:42:10.260
And for example, while I
can mention many examples,

00:42:10.260 --> 00:42:13.050
there is a book
where it says, well,

00:42:13.050 --> 00:42:16.632
how are we going to solve the
phenomenological descriptions

00:42:16.632 --> 00:42:17.840
to build a body of knowledge?

00:42:17.840 --> 00:42:22.230
It said, well, let's just
trust on human good will.

00:42:22.230 --> 00:42:24.560
And you say, well, that's great.

00:42:24.560 --> 00:42:27.340
But what next?

00:42:27.340 --> 00:42:28.900
OK.

00:42:28.900 --> 00:42:33.570
Now, the reason we
have to now ask that

00:42:33.570 --> 00:42:37.200
question in the
flip mode, and say,

00:42:37.200 --> 00:42:41.060
why is it that it has been
so much in the blind spot?

00:42:41.060 --> 00:42:45.830
And they think the reason
is that it is not easy.

00:42:45.830 --> 00:42:51.110
And it is not easy because it
takes a really solid retraining

00:42:51.110 --> 00:42:55.430
and relearning to do the
appropriate discriminations

00:42:55.430 --> 00:42:57.080
that you need to do.

00:42:57.080 --> 00:43:00.320
So the negative side is
that these things are not

00:43:00.320 --> 00:43:01.270
simple to do.

00:43:01.270 --> 00:43:03.770
Now, the positive side
is that when you do them,

00:43:03.770 --> 00:43:08.840
you have an actual rich source
of data that you can do.

00:43:08.840 --> 00:43:10.010
That you can mobilize.

00:43:10.010 --> 00:43:12.140
That you can put
into the service

00:43:12.140 --> 00:43:18.470
of a program such as the
neurophenomenology of time.

00:43:18.470 --> 00:43:19.570
OK.

00:43:19.570 --> 00:43:25.050
So what is it that
you need to work with?

00:43:25.050 --> 00:43:29.380
And here again, I'm using
both Western phenomenology

00:43:29.380 --> 00:43:33.400
and using a lot of the
phenomenology of experience

00:43:33.400 --> 00:43:36.510
as it comes down from
the Buddhist tradition.

00:43:36.510 --> 00:43:39.730
Because that's if you want--
that's their specialty.

00:43:39.730 --> 00:43:41.890
That's what they
have to contribute

00:43:41.890 --> 00:43:43.615
to the patrimony of mankind.

00:43:46.760 --> 00:43:50.480
The whole thing is,
in fact, based on a--

00:43:53.900 --> 00:43:58.220
as it should, on a very
ordinary human capacity,

00:43:58.220 --> 00:44:03.650
which is that, not surprisingly,
of being all of a sudden able

00:44:03.650 --> 00:44:08.030
of becoming aware
of, of reflecting on.

00:44:08.030 --> 00:44:09.800
Now let me make a
distinction here

00:44:09.800 --> 00:44:13.220
between reflection
and reflectiveness.

00:44:13.220 --> 00:44:16.460
Reflection is the
usual sense, a thought,

00:44:16.460 --> 00:44:20.780
thinking upon a thought or
an idea, associating an idea.

00:44:20.780 --> 00:44:23.780
What I mean about reflection
is a different kind

00:44:23.780 --> 00:44:26.420
of mental capacity
or human capacity,

00:44:26.420 --> 00:44:29.210
which is that there
is a sudden suspension

00:44:29.210 --> 00:44:31.880
of the habitual
association of thought

00:44:31.880 --> 00:44:34.100
into a mode where you say, aha.

00:44:34.100 --> 00:44:37.940
There is that discontinuity,
and you say, in English, you

00:44:37.940 --> 00:44:39.380
become aware of.

00:44:39.380 --> 00:44:41.960
All of a sudden, you can
say, you become aware of.

00:44:41.960 --> 00:44:44.330
There is a sudden interruption.

00:44:44.330 --> 00:44:47.870
That capacity for
reflectiveness is a little bit

00:44:47.870 --> 00:44:49.860
like going to a movie.

00:44:49.860 --> 00:44:53.240
You can either watch the
film go by, all of a sudden,

00:44:53.240 --> 00:44:56.340
you can step out and see,
oh, the movie's there,

00:44:56.340 --> 00:44:58.280
and this is the
color of the cinema.

00:44:58.280 --> 00:45:00.350
And you can make
some observations

00:45:00.350 --> 00:45:03.620
about the way the
film on the screen

00:45:03.620 --> 00:45:06.500
is set up, and so
on and so forth.

00:45:06.500 --> 00:45:12.170
Or if you're studying, say,
memory with a subject, you say,

00:45:12.170 --> 00:45:14.922
give me some evocations
of your past,

00:45:14.922 --> 00:45:16.880
the first thing that the
subject is going to do

00:45:16.880 --> 00:45:20.400
is to tell your
stories about his past.

00:45:20.400 --> 00:45:24.810
Very rarely he will be in a
position of actually examining

00:45:24.810 --> 00:45:30.930
what is it that he does
when he brings memory back.

00:45:30.930 --> 00:45:33.276
Not the content,
but the process.

00:45:33.276 --> 00:45:34.010
OK?

00:45:34.010 --> 00:45:39.350
That's the key to the notion
of phenomenological reduction,

00:45:39.350 --> 00:45:43.190
is to move from
content to process.

00:45:43.190 --> 00:45:45.770
To shift one level up.

00:45:45.770 --> 00:45:49.670
That particular capacity,
it is not a mysterious one.

00:45:49.670 --> 00:45:52.610
It is a very common and
ordinary human capacity.

00:45:52.610 --> 00:45:58.190
What happens is that like
for music into musicians,

00:45:58.190 --> 00:46:02.060
like from walking
into becoming skiers,

00:46:02.060 --> 00:46:03.620
it is not enough to have it.

00:46:03.620 --> 00:46:05.480
It has to be cultivated.

00:46:05.480 --> 00:46:10.020
It has to be pursued in
a very systematic way.

00:46:10.020 --> 00:46:25.490
And the cycle-- just trying
to make a decision, because

00:46:25.490 --> 00:46:27.320
of time, what to skip here.

00:46:27.320 --> 00:46:28.820
[LAUGHS] You noticed?

00:46:33.470 --> 00:46:33.970
OK.

00:46:33.970 --> 00:46:35.350
So you can say, all right.

00:46:35.350 --> 00:46:36.190
Great.

00:46:36.190 --> 00:46:39.530
I know it can reflect, so
what do I do with that?

00:46:39.530 --> 00:46:41.710
Well, both in the
phenomenological Western

00:46:41.710 --> 00:46:43.960
tradition and in the Buddhist
tradition, what you're

00:46:43.960 --> 00:46:47.650
asked to do is to
actually cultivate

00:46:47.650 --> 00:46:52.810
that to become an expert
into the following gesture,

00:46:52.810 --> 00:46:55.970
into the following fundamental
gesture, which is, on the one

00:46:55.970 --> 00:47:00.400
hand, to induce those kinds
of suspensions or breaks

00:47:00.400 --> 00:47:02.710
or bracketings.

00:47:02.710 --> 00:47:05.740
Those can be induced
either spontaneously,

00:47:05.740 --> 00:47:08.320
or you can train
yourself to induce them.

00:47:08.320 --> 00:47:10.640
That is like flipping out.

00:47:10.640 --> 00:47:13.020
The reflectiveness gesture.

00:47:13.020 --> 00:47:19.140
Then the next move is not just
to establish yourself firmly.

00:47:19.140 --> 00:47:23.490
Train that muscle, as it were,
but then to actually work

00:47:23.490 --> 00:47:27.570
with that into the
next step, which

00:47:27.570 --> 00:47:34.410
is that the process is not
just a reflection of what is it

00:47:34.410 --> 00:47:36.810
that you're looking,
but actually,

00:47:36.810 --> 00:47:40.530
you do the second
move, which is perhaps

00:47:40.530 --> 00:47:45.660
the more subtle one, which is of
letting that position of being

00:47:45.660 --> 00:47:50.150
the observer be very relaxed.

00:47:50.150 --> 00:47:53.890
This happens all the time if you
are a master skier or a master

00:47:53.890 --> 00:47:55.090
flutist.

00:47:55.090 --> 00:47:59.940
The beginner learns her
technique and is all tied up.

00:47:59.940 --> 00:48:02.460
The master is the one
who can do a gesture

00:48:02.460 --> 00:48:04.650
and then relax into it.

00:48:04.650 --> 00:48:08.130
And you cannot be a master
unless you can relax with

00:48:08.130 --> 00:48:11.470
the purposeful, or the
intentional technique.

00:48:11.470 --> 00:48:15.160
In this case, in the
case of reduction,

00:48:15.160 --> 00:48:17.740
phenomenological reduction
is exactly the same thing.

00:48:17.740 --> 00:48:19.860
You have to learn how to
induce the suspension,

00:48:19.860 --> 00:48:22.380
and then you have to
learn how to let it go.

00:48:22.380 --> 00:48:23.850
To put it down.

00:48:23.850 --> 00:48:27.830
And in that putting
it down, what happens

00:48:27.830 --> 00:48:31.520
is something that happens to
all of us, again, spontaneously,

00:48:31.520 --> 00:48:34.790
but the whole point is to
cultivate it systematically.

00:48:34.790 --> 00:48:37.740
Which is that whatever it
is that you're examining,

00:48:37.740 --> 00:48:41.810
for example, time,
can be looked at now

00:48:41.810 --> 00:48:45.440
from many different
variants or perspectives.

00:48:45.440 --> 00:48:46.270
It appears.

00:48:46.270 --> 00:48:49.920
It shows up in a fresh,
in a different way.

00:48:49.920 --> 00:48:54.530
It appears in ways in which
you were not used to seeing.

00:48:54.530 --> 00:48:56.140
And that's what
we call intuition.

00:48:56.140 --> 00:48:58.590
That's what we call insight.

00:48:58.590 --> 00:49:06.420
So as Husserl said, you build
up variance or eidetic variance

00:49:06.420 --> 00:49:11.610
on a given domain and a given
set of data, time and motion,

00:49:11.610 --> 00:49:13.050
whatever it is.

00:49:13.050 --> 00:49:15.390
And then out of that
set of variance,

00:49:15.390 --> 00:49:17.920
you can actually
extract the pattern.

00:49:17.920 --> 00:49:22.020
That's what I referred to
before as the invariances.

00:49:22.020 --> 00:49:24.390
And that gives you
the possibility

00:49:24.390 --> 00:49:29.058
of, say, building up the kinds
of diagrams that I showed.

00:49:29.058 --> 00:49:44.890
Now, As a little summary there,
so the first thing you need

00:49:44.890 --> 00:49:49.750
is the attitude of
bracketing or suspending,

00:49:49.750 --> 00:49:53.830
which leads to intuition,
the kind of intimacy

00:49:53.830 --> 00:49:58.540
of direct evidence that is
proper to a direct examination.

00:49:58.540 --> 00:50:02.260
Which leads to
invariance, that can enter

00:50:02.260 --> 00:50:05.410
into next-level descriptions.

00:50:05.410 --> 00:50:09.610
But none of that is possible
without a very solid training.

00:50:09.610 --> 00:50:12.040
The stability, the
pragmatics that

00:50:12.040 --> 00:50:16.030
needs to develop over
the course of time.

00:50:16.030 --> 00:50:18.310
Now, it is said in the
Buddhist tradition,

00:50:18.310 --> 00:50:23.380
for example, that although
it is easy to start,

00:50:23.380 --> 00:50:31.380
it is very hard to become
a real fluid practitioner.

00:50:31.380 --> 00:50:36.050
And by practitioner here, all we
mean is a good phenomenologist.

00:50:36.050 --> 00:50:38.090
The reason, again, is simple.

00:50:38.090 --> 00:50:40.640
Anything that you
need to learn requires

00:50:40.640 --> 00:50:42.620
that kind of engagement.

00:50:42.620 --> 00:50:49.070
And it just, again, befuddles me
why is it that it appears that

00:50:49.070 --> 00:50:52.670
we should become instant experts
about our own experience when

00:50:52.670 --> 00:50:56.060
we know we cannot be instant
experts about anything,

00:50:56.060 --> 00:50:57.620
just about.

00:50:57.620 --> 00:51:00.750
We all need that
kind of engagement.

00:51:00.750 --> 00:51:05.070
But suppose that we have
the interest and the stamina

00:51:05.070 --> 00:51:05.970
and the guts--

00:51:05.970 --> 00:51:09.330
by the way, Husserl
was a real exception,

00:51:09.330 --> 00:51:12.750
Because you can say, where
did he learn it from?

00:51:12.750 --> 00:51:15.300
Well, if you read
his material, it's

00:51:15.300 --> 00:51:17.220
amazing how much
he actually-- you

00:51:17.220 --> 00:51:20.670
can see this kind of
reduction in action.

00:51:20.670 --> 00:51:23.370
He not only came up with
fantastic descriptions

00:51:23.370 --> 00:51:26.760
about time, he also anticipated
a whole bunch of things

00:51:26.760 --> 00:51:29.610
that we know today in
neuroscience about space,

00:51:29.610 --> 00:51:33.730
about affection, affect, I
should say, emotionality,

00:51:33.730 --> 00:51:36.290
intersubjectivity, and a
whole bunch of other things.

00:51:36.290 --> 00:51:37.870
You say, how did he do it?

00:51:37.870 --> 00:51:41.460
Well, it seems that Husserl, you
can see it in action, in fact,

00:51:41.460 --> 00:51:44.670
he was probably a
very gifted person,

00:51:44.670 --> 00:51:52.390
like, if I might say so, a bit
of the Mozart of reduction.

00:51:52.390 --> 00:51:58.020
Now, not all of us have that
kind of luck, and most of us

00:51:58.020 --> 00:51:59.600
have to work hard.

00:51:59.600 --> 00:52:01.050
And working hard for what?

00:52:01.050 --> 00:52:02.770
Well, it depends on
what you want to do.

00:52:02.770 --> 00:52:06.750
But if you're interested, I
say, in neurophenomenology,

00:52:06.750 --> 00:52:09.330
that kind of training
is necessary in order

00:52:09.330 --> 00:52:12.840
to actually understand how
to mobilize the resources

00:52:12.840 --> 00:52:13.830
of your own experience.

00:52:13.830 --> 00:52:16.200
But there is another motivation.

00:52:16.200 --> 00:52:20.310
And that's where the Buddhist
angle comes into here.

00:52:20.310 --> 00:52:22.484
And don't worry, I
will end in time.

00:52:22.484 --> 00:52:24.340
[LAUGHS]

00:52:25.270 --> 00:52:32.410
Which is that when you take this
attitude to your own experience

00:52:32.410 --> 00:52:37.690
of seeing that whatever it
is that comes up in everyday,

00:52:37.690 --> 00:52:41.470
ordinary experience can be
deepened, can be explored,

00:52:41.470 --> 00:52:45.940
can be unfolded, can be enfolded
by applying a disciplined,

00:52:45.940 --> 00:52:49.320
sustained cultivation.

00:52:49.320 --> 00:52:51.620
Yes, you can do some
interesting research.

00:52:51.620 --> 00:52:56.640
I hope that I have at least
suggested that direction.

00:52:56.640 --> 00:53:02.580
But also, again, inseparable
from that discovery

00:53:02.580 --> 00:53:04.800
of the structure
of experience, you

00:53:04.800 --> 00:53:09.100
begin to discover a few
things about yourself.

00:53:09.100 --> 00:53:11.520
In other words, it
touches what you

00:53:11.520 --> 00:53:14.160
might call the existential
dimension of experience.

00:53:14.160 --> 00:53:16.500
And that's where, in
the Buddhist tradition,

00:53:16.500 --> 00:53:18.880
there are not really
scientists in that sense.

00:53:18.880 --> 00:53:21.150
They're not interested
in empirical accounts,

00:53:21.150 --> 00:53:23.340
although they might,
in fact, they are,

00:53:23.340 --> 00:53:25.110
but not true historically.

00:53:25.110 --> 00:53:27.850
They're much more
interested in how

00:53:27.850 --> 00:53:31.560
that kind of training,
that kind of discipline

00:53:31.560 --> 00:53:34.560
leads to a human transformation.

00:53:34.560 --> 00:53:36.120
Leads to a spiritual quest.

00:53:39.620 --> 00:53:42.250
So you can say, well, tell me.

00:53:42.250 --> 00:53:45.890
How does it lead?

00:53:45.890 --> 00:53:49.530
Again, let me give
you an example.

00:53:49.530 --> 00:53:54.160
When you carry on the process of
systematic, sustained reduction

00:53:54.160 --> 00:53:59.150
into the observation, for
example, of the question,

00:53:59.150 --> 00:54:02.660
how is your own
identity constituted?

00:54:02.660 --> 00:54:05.660
What is it to be a you?

00:54:05.660 --> 00:54:09.050
Not what is it to be a bat,
but what is it to be a you,

00:54:09.050 --> 00:54:11.487
what is it to be a me?

00:54:11.487 --> 00:54:13.070
One of the first
things that comes up,

00:54:13.070 --> 00:54:16.230
and it's considered to
be sort of Buddhism 101,

00:54:16.230 --> 00:54:23.510
or practitioner 101 inside, and
it's readily confirmed whenever

00:54:23.510 --> 00:54:27.620
you engage sufficiently
in this kind of training,

00:54:27.620 --> 00:54:31.310
is that what is interesting
about the nature of myself,

00:54:31.310 --> 00:54:33.950
when I say, what is it myself?

00:54:33.950 --> 00:54:35.660
Where do I find it?

00:54:35.660 --> 00:54:36.980
How do I find it?

00:54:36.980 --> 00:54:38.240
What is its nature?

00:54:38.240 --> 00:54:40.240
What is its texture?

00:54:40.240 --> 00:54:43.360
Is that under
eidetic variations,

00:54:43.360 --> 00:54:45.610
when you submit that
process repeatedly

00:54:45.610 --> 00:54:51.340
under examination, what you see
is that the nature of the self,

00:54:51.340 --> 00:54:56.420
as it is said, is precisely
that it is unfindable.

00:54:56.420 --> 00:55:01.880
The nature of its unfindability,
it is a sudden insight--

00:55:01.880 --> 00:55:03.650
insight, intuition?

00:55:03.650 --> 00:55:07.550
You get this new
realization which

00:55:07.550 --> 00:55:10.390
is not particularly abstract.

00:55:10.390 --> 00:55:11.780
It is very intuitive.

00:55:11.780 --> 00:55:12.740
It is very direct.

00:55:12.740 --> 00:55:14.000
It is very intimate.

00:55:14.000 --> 00:55:16.070
Into what?

00:55:16.070 --> 00:55:20.510
Into a realm of phenomena
which is quite radical.

00:55:20.510 --> 00:55:24.160
Which is the non-findability.

00:55:24.160 --> 00:55:26.710
In the old days,
you used to say,

00:55:26.710 --> 00:55:29.950
people used to translate this
non-findability of saying,

00:55:29.950 --> 00:55:31.510
no self.

00:55:31.510 --> 00:55:33.280
Well, it's not quite no self.

00:55:33.280 --> 00:55:35.740
It is unfindable self.

00:55:35.740 --> 00:55:37.060
And you can say, oh, yes.

00:55:37.060 --> 00:55:37.780
I understand.

00:55:37.780 --> 00:55:40.300
Myself is just an
association of stories

00:55:40.300 --> 00:55:41.450
that I'm telling myself.

00:55:41.450 --> 00:55:43.780
And you can have a
very elaborate theory

00:55:43.780 --> 00:55:46.450
about how your self
is a composite entity.

00:55:46.450 --> 00:55:47.770
Many scientists do.

00:55:47.770 --> 00:55:51.440
In fact, lots of people who
write upon consciousness do.

00:55:51.440 --> 00:55:53.030
That's not the point.

00:55:53.030 --> 00:55:55.450
The point is why
is it that we'll

00:55:55.450 --> 00:56:01.210
continue to behave as if we
had a very solid reference

00:56:01.210 --> 00:56:06.430
and a very solid fixed point
of view to defend, to maintain,

00:56:06.430 --> 00:56:11.280
to sustain, to
distinguish, and to create

00:56:11.280 --> 00:56:14.770
in the ultimate analysis,
a whole bunch of trouble

00:56:14.770 --> 00:56:15.860
for those around us.

00:56:20.370 --> 00:56:24.420
The point here is that
this kind of examination,

00:56:24.420 --> 00:56:27.560
it's not the one
that it is purely

00:56:27.560 --> 00:56:29.280
the intellectual analysis.

00:56:29.280 --> 00:56:32.010
The intellectual analysis
leads to the motivation

00:56:32.010 --> 00:56:34.500
into the sustained
examination that

00:56:34.500 --> 00:56:37.680
leads to an insight
that is transformative.

00:56:37.680 --> 00:56:42.840
That, to me, the core
of the spiritual path.

00:56:42.840 --> 00:56:46.500
The spiritual because
the direct intuition,

00:56:46.500 --> 00:56:49.450
the direct examination
actually changes

00:56:49.450 --> 00:56:53.220
you, which doesn't
necessarily do the mere

00:56:53.220 --> 00:56:58.170
or the pure logical analysis.

00:56:58.170 --> 00:57:03.300
There's an old text from
the 15th century where

00:57:03.300 --> 00:57:05.880
a man called Shekbur said,
"not finding anything

00:57:05.880 --> 00:57:08.380
is an incredible find."

00:57:08.380 --> 00:57:13.090
Or actually, the
Dalai Lama these days,

00:57:13.090 --> 00:57:17.080
he uses a very quaint
colloquial Tibetan expression

00:57:17.080 --> 00:57:18.580
to express the same idea.

00:57:18.580 --> 00:57:23.050
He says,
[SPEAKING TIBETAN] which

00:57:23.050 --> 00:57:27.360
means, literally, "Nothing
where my finger can go.

00:57:27.360 --> 00:57:31.140
There is no place to
put my finger on."

00:57:31.140 --> 00:57:38.170
Now, I'm trying here to
make you have the sense

00:57:38.170 --> 00:57:42.400
that it is quite different
to say that, which

00:57:42.400 --> 00:57:44.540
is an understandable statement.

00:57:44.540 --> 00:57:47.050
And you can even
analyze it and be

00:57:47.050 --> 00:57:49.980
very smart about the
logical composition

00:57:49.980 --> 00:57:52.870
of that kind of
sentences and the fact

00:57:52.870 --> 00:57:56.440
that that kind of statement
contains an existential impact

00:57:56.440 --> 00:58:02.130
once it becomes yours by that
kind of immediate knowledge.

00:58:02.130 --> 00:58:05.510
So that's the main point, here.

00:58:05.510 --> 00:58:09.960
That it is the existential
impact of that kind of analysis

00:58:09.960 --> 00:58:15.660
that creates the opening
into the spiritual dimension.

00:58:15.660 --> 00:58:23.160
So let me try and get to the
roundup of my presentation

00:58:23.160 --> 00:58:30.120
by saying, I gave you, I
hope, the argument to say

00:58:30.120 --> 00:58:34.200
that the nature of experience
is way more interesting than we

00:58:34.200 --> 00:58:35.340
think.

00:58:35.340 --> 00:58:39.750
On the one hand, I think
we can do a pretty good job

00:58:39.750 --> 00:58:43.980
at understanding experience
from a perfectly reasonable

00:58:43.980 --> 00:58:47.880
scientific point of view
if we avail ourselves

00:58:47.880 --> 00:58:50.160
of the neurophenomenological
perspective

00:58:50.160 --> 00:58:52.920
as a research program.

00:58:52.920 --> 00:58:57.420
And if we avail ourselves
with the proper training

00:58:57.420 --> 00:59:00.810
and the proper methods
to actually exploit

00:59:00.810 --> 00:59:07.010
what's in there, once those
two things are in place,

00:59:07.010 --> 00:59:10.460
I personally see
absolutely no problem

00:59:10.460 --> 00:59:14.420
or no conflict between
science and spirituality.

00:59:14.420 --> 00:59:17.240
Because in fact, we're
talking about the same thing.

00:59:17.240 --> 00:59:19.640
We're talking about
what is contained

00:59:19.640 --> 00:59:21.080
in the nature of experience.

00:59:21.080 --> 00:59:23.870
You can take that nature of
the richness of that experience

00:59:23.870 --> 00:59:28.130
into a good bit
of science, or you

00:59:28.130 --> 00:59:31.430
can take it into a
transformative path

00:59:31.430 --> 00:59:32.960
for your own life.

00:59:32.960 --> 00:59:37.230
That's, of course, not a
contradiction in terms at all.

00:59:37.230 --> 00:59:42.230
And once that's, it's to
me, established a frame

00:59:42.230 --> 00:59:45.820
or as a possibility,
what I can--

00:59:45.820 --> 00:59:48.410
inevitably, you need to--

00:59:48.410 --> 00:59:54.310
I think we have to conclude is
that the naturalistic events,

00:59:54.310 --> 00:59:57.760
the brains, the bodies,
the neurons, seen

00:59:57.760 --> 00:59:59.350
from the point of
view of the more

00:59:59.350 --> 01:00:03.370
existentially deep understanding
of the nature of self,

01:00:03.370 --> 01:00:06.310
for example, acquire
a transcendence.

01:00:06.310 --> 01:00:08.860
The neurons, all of a sudden,
are not just these little blips

01:00:08.860 --> 01:00:11.830
out there, yet they are.

01:00:11.830 --> 01:00:15.760
They are not denying that
there are calcium-dependent,

01:00:15.760 --> 01:00:20.110
potassium-dependent voltage
currents and so on and so

01:00:20.110 --> 01:00:21.910
forth.

01:00:21.910 --> 01:00:26.850
but there is a communication, a
translucency or a transparency,

01:00:26.850 --> 01:00:29.460
into the more
existential dimension.

01:00:29.460 --> 01:00:35.040
At the same time, the purely
very indescribable, sometimes,

01:00:35.040 --> 01:00:37.630
existential depths of
one's own experience,

01:00:37.630 --> 01:00:41.160
which is sometimes unsayable,
because unfindable,

01:00:41.160 --> 01:00:45.990
finds a very natural connection
into the roots of nature

01:00:45.990 --> 01:00:48.730
and into what
science is all about.

01:00:48.730 --> 01:00:52.970
So from that point of view, the
title that I gave to this talk,

01:00:52.970 --> 01:00:55.800
it's why a proper
science of mine, i.e.

01:00:55.800 --> 01:00:58.620
a proper witness
to new resources

01:00:58.620 --> 01:01:02.940
that we need to put in, implies
the transcendence of nature.

01:01:02.940 --> 01:01:06.150
That is, where the
material and the spiritual

01:01:06.150 --> 01:01:10.626
are certainly non-dual, but
transparent to each other.

01:01:10.626 --> 01:01:11.598
Thank you.

01:01:23.494 --> 01:01:24.660
Two minutes late, I'm sorry.

01:01:24.660 --> 01:01:26.285
PROFESSOR: Thanks
very much, Francisco.

01:01:26.285 --> 01:01:28.915
We have-- actually, we
have nearly 15 minutes

01:01:28.915 --> 01:01:29.540
for a question.

01:01:29.540 --> 01:01:31.020
We have, definitely, 10 minutes.

01:01:31.020 --> 01:01:33.162
So do you want to take
the questions, or--

01:01:33.162 --> 01:01:34.120
FRANCISCO VARELA: Sure.

01:01:34.120 --> 01:01:34.250
PROFESSOR: Yeah?

01:01:34.250 --> 01:01:34.620
OK.

01:01:34.620 --> 01:01:35.300
FRANCISCO VARELA: No, please.

01:01:35.300 --> 01:01:35.807
You--

01:01:35.807 --> 01:01:36.390
PROFESSOR: OK.

01:01:36.390 --> 01:01:37.170
FRANCISCO VARELA:
You be the chairman.

01:01:37.170 --> 01:01:38.128
PROFESSOR: Yes, please.

01:01:38.128 --> 01:01:41.494
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:01:50.294 --> 01:01:51.210
FRANCISCO VARELA: Yes.

01:01:51.210 --> 01:01:52.120
Thank you.

01:01:52.120 --> 01:01:53.180
It's a great question.

01:01:53.180 --> 01:01:56.360
Because indeed, like
there is different kinds

01:01:56.360 --> 01:01:58.110
of little self-analysis
in science

01:01:58.110 --> 01:02:00.380
and different
motivations for doing it,

01:02:00.380 --> 01:02:02.130
there are different
levels of analysis

01:02:02.130 --> 01:02:04.580
in the Buddhist phenomenology.

01:02:04.580 --> 01:02:07.200
There is, at least, I
would say, two extremes

01:02:07.200 --> 01:02:09.540
and the whole
gradation in between.

01:02:09.540 --> 01:02:13.710
One is the kind of
tradition in Buddhism

01:02:13.710 --> 01:02:16.140
which gives rise to the
whole corpus of say,

01:02:16.140 --> 01:02:19.490
the so-called Abidharma
tradition, which is the detail,

01:02:19.490 --> 01:02:22.930
I would say today,
phenomenological psychology.

01:02:22.930 --> 01:02:24.530
The components of experience.

01:02:24.530 --> 01:02:26.390
This is very minute appearing.

01:02:26.390 --> 01:02:28.520
How many emotions
can we discriminate?

01:02:28.520 --> 01:02:30.320
How does time appear?

01:02:30.320 --> 01:02:32.960
What is the nature
of, for example,

01:02:32.960 --> 01:02:36.950
the perception of the other,
and so on and so forth.

01:02:36.950 --> 01:02:40.860
That leads very much into a
kind of a scientific dimension,

01:02:40.860 --> 01:02:42.680
and that's where
it's easy to have

01:02:42.680 --> 01:02:46.550
a dialogue between cognitive
neuroscience and Buddhism.

01:02:46.550 --> 01:02:51.410
Then there is the more
existential schools

01:02:51.410 --> 01:02:54.650
or traditions, such as
the so-called [INAUDIBLE]

01:02:54.650 --> 01:02:57.699
or Mahamudra traditions
in the Tibetan school,

01:02:57.699 --> 01:02:59.240
where, in fact, the
whole emphasis is

01:02:59.240 --> 01:03:02.780
much more on non-findability
and that idea of non-findability

01:03:02.780 --> 01:03:05.120
or as it is
sometimes translated,

01:03:05.120 --> 01:03:09.320
as emptiness or sunyata, in
the original Sanskrit term,

01:03:09.320 --> 01:03:13.120
is taken into more and
more radical degrees.

01:03:13.120 --> 01:03:15.470
There is progressive
stages in the understanding

01:03:15.470 --> 01:03:17.060
of that emptiness.

01:03:17.060 --> 01:03:19.910
The literal example vignette
that I mentioned today

01:03:19.910 --> 01:03:23.210
about the
non-findability of myself

01:03:23.210 --> 01:03:28.010
is but one stage, and that
can be cultivated further,

01:03:28.010 --> 01:03:33.710
like anything else, into
a real, if you want,

01:03:33.710 --> 01:03:39.020
naked form of non-perception
or emptiness, where, of course,

01:03:39.020 --> 01:03:42.320
you have to choose your
style, and you have to choose

01:03:42.320 --> 01:03:46.177
your fate and your preferences.

01:03:46.177 --> 01:03:47.093
PROFESSOR: Yes please.

01:03:47.093 --> 01:03:47.745
Morgan.

01:03:47.745 --> 01:03:49.578
AUDIENCE: I just had
two technical questions

01:03:49.578 --> 01:03:51.080
about your--

01:03:51.080 --> 01:03:52.056
the brain evidence.

01:03:52.056 --> 01:03:55.940
The first one was when you
showed the information where

01:03:55.940 --> 01:03:58.409
you did the statistical
correlations,

01:03:58.409 --> 01:04:00.450
you mentioned that those
were epileptic patients.

01:04:00.450 --> 01:04:02.242
But did they have their
[INAUDIBLE] intact?

01:04:02.242 --> 01:04:03.158
FRANCISCO VARELA: Yes.

01:04:03.158 --> 01:04:04.290
AUDIENCE: OK.

01:04:04.290 --> 01:04:09.760
And the second one was the
mirror particle neurons.

01:04:13.900 --> 01:04:16.050
Can you give us a pointer
to that work, or--

01:04:16.050 --> 01:04:16.966
FRANCISCO VARELA: Yes.

01:04:16.966 --> 01:04:21.996
You just look at
Rizzolatti, Science, 1995.

01:04:21.996 --> 01:04:24.364
AUDIENCE: Are those the
interior parietal lobule?

01:04:24.364 --> 01:04:26.530
FRANCISCO VARELA: Yes, the
interior parietal lobule.

01:04:26.530 --> 01:04:28.432
Correct.

01:04:28.432 --> 01:04:29.390
PROFESSOR: Yes, please.

01:04:32.040 --> 01:04:34.970
AUDIENCE: Professor Brooks
said in another lecture

01:04:34.970 --> 01:04:40.300
that there's no subjectivity,
if I understood him correctly.

01:04:40.300 --> 01:04:44.820
Now you are saying
there is no self.

01:04:44.820 --> 01:04:47.640
Now, I think that this is a
critically important thing,

01:04:47.640 --> 01:04:53.850
if we could go into this
a little more slowly.

01:04:53.850 --> 01:05:00.670
Could you tell-- when
is your examination

01:05:00.670 --> 01:05:02.550
of the self exhausted?

01:05:02.550 --> 01:05:05.590
That is to say, did
you go into the nature

01:05:05.590 --> 01:05:10.360
of this eidetic reduction,
for example, you are saying,

01:05:10.360 --> 01:05:14.140
at some point, you are
certain that there is no self.

01:05:14.140 --> 01:05:18.370
Or what is the nature of
exhausting this examination

01:05:18.370 --> 01:05:19.041
to find--

01:05:19.041 --> 01:05:21.200
FRANCISCO VARELA:
There is no exhaustion.

01:05:21.200 --> 01:05:23.237
It's an open question,
as far as I'm concerned.

01:05:23.237 --> 01:05:25.320
AUDIENCE: How can you state
that there is no self?

01:05:25.320 --> 01:05:26.195
FRANCISCO VARELA: No.

01:05:26.195 --> 01:05:28.270
I state that there is no self--

01:05:28.270 --> 01:05:30.070
first of all, I
didn't say no self.

01:05:30.070 --> 01:05:33.340
I qualified that by saying
that the self is not findable.

01:05:33.340 --> 01:05:34.180
Very different.

01:05:34.180 --> 01:05:35.350
We do have a self.

01:05:35.350 --> 01:05:36.201
I'm Francisco.

01:05:36.201 --> 01:05:36.700
Hi.

01:05:36.700 --> 01:05:38.530
How are you?

01:05:38.530 --> 01:05:40.360
I have no problem with that.

01:05:40.360 --> 01:05:43.720
Nobody is trying to convince
anybody you don't exist.

01:05:43.720 --> 01:05:48.670
The question is what is the
nature of that existence?

01:05:48.670 --> 01:05:51.700
We have a mode of
existence that deserves

01:05:51.700 --> 01:05:55.570
a really precise answer.

01:05:55.570 --> 01:05:59.290
And what the examination and
phenomenological analysis

01:05:59.290 --> 01:06:02.800
yields, at the first level,
is something quite simple.

01:06:02.800 --> 01:06:05.620
That is that this composite--

01:06:05.620 --> 01:06:07.200
first of all, it is a composite.

01:06:07.200 --> 01:06:10.290
But we can also deduce
that even from science.

01:06:10.290 --> 01:06:12.840
And second, that
it is not findable.

01:06:12.840 --> 01:06:15.470
You cannot put your
finger, as Dalai Lama says,

01:06:15.470 --> 01:06:17.870
you cannot put your finger in.

01:06:17.870 --> 01:06:20.910
Now, you can say, what gives
you the right to say that?

01:06:20.910 --> 01:06:23.700
It gives me the right, the
same right that anybody

01:06:23.700 --> 01:06:25.740
who does a good
experiment, who says,

01:06:25.740 --> 01:06:27.600
these are my conditions
and observations.

01:06:27.600 --> 01:06:30.240
I go, and this is my method.

01:06:30.240 --> 01:06:31.830
This is what I see.

01:06:31.830 --> 01:06:34.110
That's what I've been
trying to propose to you.

01:06:34.110 --> 01:06:37.470
I don't think that that's final.

01:06:37.470 --> 01:06:40.770
It is one proposition
for examination

01:06:40.770 --> 01:06:43.620
where we're trying to be
honest with each other and say,

01:06:43.620 --> 01:06:44.860
this is how I do it.

01:06:44.860 --> 01:06:46.730
This is what I see.

01:06:46.730 --> 01:06:49.170
Why don't you check?

01:06:49.170 --> 01:06:50.680
I know that you have.

01:06:50.680 --> 01:06:54.740
[LAUGHS] But what I mean is
it's the same kind of routine

01:06:54.740 --> 01:06:56.310
as any kind of examination.

01:06:56.310 --> 01:06:58.590
So yes, I can say it.

01:06:58.590 --> 01:07:02.060
Do I hold to that as some
kind of ultimate truth?

01:07:02.060 --> 01:07:03.050
No, of course not.

01:07:03.050 --> 01:07:06.715
To me, the whole thing
is an open question.

01:07:06.715 --> 01:07:08.340
PROFESSOR: And I also
would like to add

01:07:08.340 --> 01:07:10.290
that Rod didn't say
that there is not

01:07:10.290 --> 01:07:11.970
such a thing like subjectivity.

01:07:11.970 --> 01:07:14.640
He said, it's not something
to distinguish machines

01:07:14.640 --> 01:07:16.770
from humans.

01:07:16.770 --> 01:07:18.420
That computers can
have that as well.

01:07:18.420 --> 01:07:20.970
But he never said anything
like that there is not

01:07:20.970 --> 01:07:22.540
such a thing like subjectivity.

01:07:22.540 --> 01:07:24.290
Just to clarify for
the people who haven't

01:07:24.290 --> 01:07:25.456
been at Rodney Brooks' talk.

01:07:25.456 --> 01:07:28.620
I wouldn't like him to
be presented in that--

01:07:28.620 --> 01:07:30.699
yes, please.

01:07:30.699 --> 01:07:32.865
AUDIENCE: I'm really
fascinated with a lot of things

01:07:32.865 --> 01:07:33.330
that you said.

01:07:33.330 --> 01:07:34.410
And I'm thinking
in the life of--

01:07:34.410 --> 01:07:35.780
FRANCISCO VARELA: I think
you'll have to speak up

01:07:35.780 --> 01:07:36.905
for the people in the back.

01:07:36.905 --> 01:07:39.850
AUDIENCE: I'm thinking, in
the life of a scientist,

01:07:39.850 --> 01:07:45.525
in my case, a neuropsychologist,
my colleagues are

01:07:45.525 --> 01:07:49.336
of the same bent, of
life is divided up

01:07:49.336 --> 01:07:51.252
into being very cautionistic
in the laboratory

01:07:51.252 --> 01:07:52.250
to find the answers.

01:07:52.250 --> 01:07:58.840
And many will take weekends,
days, evenings, or weeks

01:07:58.840 --> 01:08:02.230
off to get in touch
with themselves.

01:08:02.230 --> 01:08:08.605
To meditate, to revive
themselves so that they

01:08:08.605 --> 01:08:11.970
can go back to do more
reductionistic work.

01:08:11.970 --> 01:08:16.040
The point being that this
separating the phenomenological

01:08:16.040 --> 01:08:19.612
with the reductionistic,
where you

01:08:19.612 --> 01:08:22.853
seem to be saying-- at
least I hope you're saying--

01:08:22.853 --> 01:08:24.520
is that trying to
integrate that,

01:08:24.520 --> 01:08:28.192
bringing that sense in the
morning, in the evening,

01:08:28.192 --> 01:08:31.292
in the laboratory if
possible, because they really

01:08:31.292 --> 01:08:32.010
do correlate.

01:08:32.010 --> 01:08:32.196
FRANCISCO VARELA: Thank you.

01:08:32.196 --> 01:08:33.640
That's precisely the point.

01:08:33.640 --> 01:08:36.189
That I must be
stupid or something,

01:08:36.189 --> 01:08:40.600
but I fail to see why is it
that these two things should

01:08:40.600 --> 01:08:42.260
be in conflict.

01:08:42.260 --> 01:08:44.210
Now, they are in conflict.

01:08:44.210 --> 01:08:46.850
We just hear the way
we have been taught

01:08:46.850 --> 01:08:48.989
or we've been trained
because of the assumptions

01:08:48.989 --> 01:08:51.580
that we've been carrying on
from the history of science

01:08:51.580 --> 01:08:53.680
and the history of
Western tradition.

01:08:53.680 --> 01:08:54.910
That's fine.

01:08:54.910 --> 01:08:57.920
Nobody says we
have to stay there.

01:08:57.920 --> 01:09:02.170
And what I'm trying to say,
just to complement what you just

01:09:02.170 --> 01:09:05.710
pointed out, is that it
is not enough to notice

01:09:05.710 --> 01:09:06.700
that this is happening.

01:09:06.700 --> 01:09:09.939
We have to avail
ourselves with new tools

01:09:09.939 --> 01:09:14.770
to actually produce the change
so that this interpenetration

01:09:14.770 --> 01:09:16.229
can happen.

01:09:16.229 --> 01:09:20.470
Now, it seems to me, stands
to be absolutely to reason

01:09:20.470 --> 01:09:25.390
that if you're studying brains
and minds and psychology,

01:09:25.390 --> 01:09:28.300
that kind of presence
of experience,

01:09:28.300 --> 01:09:32.560
both in its mechanism and
in its existential depths,

01:09:32.560 --> 01:09:33.910
it's inescapable.

01:09:33.910 --> 01:09:35.680
We're bound to find it.

01:09:35.680 --> 01:09:37.994
We try to chase it
out through the door,

01:09:37.994 --> 01:09:40.160
and it comes back through
the window, as it is said.

01:09:40.160 --> 01:09:42.220
And in the history
of psychology,

01:09:42.220 --> 01:09:46.300
it's a compulsive repetition
of the same kind of gesture.

01:09:46.300 --> 01:09:48.040
So yes.

01:09:48.040 --> 01:09:53.290
That is very much
the kind of vision

01:09:53.290 --> 01:09:55.840
that I'm trying to develop.

01:09:55.840 --> 01:09:59.690
And again, I must say, I've
been very inspired in this

01:09:59.690 --> 01:10:01.150
by the Buddhist tradition.

01:10:01.150 --> 01:10:04.960
Because in fact, Buddhism,
as the question before

01:10:04.960 --> 01:10:07.720
pointed out, is very
diverse, but in its spirit,

01:10:07.720 --> 01:10:09.080
it's very much that.

01:10:09.080 --> 01:10:12.570
It's the ordinary,
but the ordinary,

01:10:12.570 --> 01:10:17.630
but the entire depth
of ordinariness.

01:10:17.630 --> 01:10:22.474
And so morning, evening,
and weekends, no different.

01:10:22.474 --> 01:10:24.390
PROFESSOR: We have time
for one more question.

01:10:24.390 --> 01:10:26.008
Yes, please.

01:10:26.008 --> 01:10:28.004
AUDIENCE: I'm
comfortable with the idea

01:10:28.004 --> 01:10:30.499
that computers have
computational lives.

01:10:30.499 --> 01:10:35.072
What about the idea that they
have phenomenological lives?

01:10:35.072 --> 01:10:35.988
FRANCISCO VARELA: Rod.

01:10:35.988 --> 01:10:38.570
[LAUGHTER]

01:10:38.570 --> 01:10:40.361
PROFESSOR: Rod, do you
want to answer that?

01:10:40.361 --> 01:10:42.205
[LAUGHTER]

01:10:42.205 --> 01:10:44.622
AUDIENCE: My answer is obvious
from my talk two weeks ago.

01:10:44.622 --> 01:10:45.496
FRANCISCO VARELA: No.

01:10:45.496 --> 01:10:46.740
I was just being facetious.

01:10:46.740 --> 01:10:49.970
I'll be very
straightforward here.

01:10:49.970 --> 01:10:52.510
I don't know.

01:10:52.510 --> 01:10:54.160
In other words, what
do I mean by no,

01:10:54.160 --> 01:10:56.980
no, it's not just a way of
avoiding the question is it

01:10:56.980 --> 01:10:59.780
seems to me an open question.

01:10:59.780 --> 01:11:08.400
In principle, I see no objection
or no logical or a priori

01:11:08.400 --> 01:11:11.760
reasoning or conclusion
or observation that

01:11:11.760 --> 01:11:14.620
would lead me to rule that out.

01:11:14.620 --> 01:11:18.270
So a cog might, one
day, in its fifth, 10th,

01:11:18.270 --> 01:11:23.790
or 40th generation,
become aware and have

01:11:23.790 --> 01:11:26.190
the same kind of empty self.

01:11:26.190 --> 01:11:28.190
Who knows?

01:11:28.190 --> 01:11:29.470
We don't know.

01:11:29.470 --> 01:11:34.742
There is-- it's
an open question.

01:11:34.742 --> 01:11:36.700
PROFESSOR: Unfortunately,
we have to stop here.

01:11:36.700 --> 01:11:37.590
I'm very sorry.

01:11:37.590 --> 01:11:38.465
FRANCISCO VARELA: OK.

01:11:38.465 --> 01:11:40.640
Thanks for having me.

